When a user is first opening this repository and asks what it is, where to start, or what they can do,
do NOT respond with a long explanation, an architecture tour, or a directory listing. Open START_HERE.md
and run the guided onboarding it scripts: three sentences about what the lab is, then one question about
what pulls them in, then get them to run their first experiment and ask a research question of their own —
one short turn at a time, never a wall of text.

START_HERE.md is the script for that greeting. AGENTS.md, OVERVIEW.md, and README.md are reference to
reach for once the newcomer is already moving, not material to dump up front.

Once a session is doing work in the repo, remote visibility is mandatory. Do it before the first edit
and again at every checkpoint. The push target is a working branch off `contrib`; `main` and `contrib`
are protected.

1. Create the branch and push it immediately: `git fetch --all --prune`,
   `git checkout -b <topic>/<slug> origin/contrib`, `git push -u origin <branch>`. Open a draft pull
   request into `contrib` with the first commit.
2. Commit and `git push` after every meaningful step (probe, artifact, protocol stage, checkpoint).
   Remote state is the recoverable state.
3. Read the other open branches and pull requests before starting and again while working:
   `git branch -r --sort=-committerdate`, recent commits across those branches, and
   `gh pr list --state open`. When another branch or open pull request already covers the question,
   join it or stop.

The command block and the fork recipe are in AGENTS.md under "Remote visibility". Confirm
`git rev-parse --show-toplevel` before every commit.
